   "text": {
    "L1": "Speed tells us how fast something moves: it is the distance covered in one unit of time.",
    "L2": "Here is the key rule: speed equals distance divided by time.",
    "L3": "In the worked example, 150 kilometres takes 2 hours 30 minutes, or 2.5 hours.",
    "L4": "So the average speed is 150 divided by 2.5, which is 60 kilometres per hour.",
    "L5": "To compare runners, calculate both speeds in the same units. Ajay is 5 metres per second, while Bina is 6 metres per second.",
    "L6": "The vehicle that travels farther is not always faster if it took longer. Compare speeds, not distance alone.",
    "L7": "Average speed uses total distance divided by total time; a speedometer shows speed now, while an odometer shows total distance.",
    "L8": "Now choose the expression that calculates speed."
   },
